Relevant columns and their potential usage
• ball_by_ball.match_id :   links every ball to its match – the main grain of the analysis  
• ball_by_ball.over_id / ball_by_ball.ball_id / ball_by_ball.innings_no :   together with match_id make a “ball‑key” that lets us join all the per‑delivery tables  
• ball_by_ball.striker :   first member of every batting pair (one of the two ids we need)  
• ball_by_ball.non_striker :   second member of every batting pair  
• batsman_scored.runs_scored :   runs credited to the striker on that ball – used for each player’s individual score inside a partnership  
• extra_runs.extra_runs :   runs not credited to a batsman but that still belong to the partnership total  
• wicket_taken.* :   can be used to delimit partnerships if we wanted the “true” continuous partnership, although not strictly needed when we just sum runs for every appearance of the same pair in a match  
• player.player_id :   gives the numeric id that will finally be reported  
Conditions that will be useful  
• join ball_by_ball  ↔ batsman_scored  ON (match_id, over_id, ball_id, innings_no)  
• join ball_by_ball  ↔ extra_runs      ON (match_id, over_id, ball_id, innings_no)  
• ball_by_ball.striker  ≠  ball_by_ball.non_striker   (always true, but guarantees we are dealing with two different players)  
• for partnership ordering inside a pair:  
     if player1_runs > player2_runs → player1 = hitter with more runs  
     if the two runs are equal        → player with HIGHER id is player1  


Below are 10 exploratory SQL queries (each limited to 20 rows) that go from very simple inspections to a compact version of the final answer.  
Every query is preceded by a short description.

